Notebook[{
Cell["MATHEMATICA ROGRAMMING", "Title"],

Cell["Rule Based Programming", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Another definition for the factorial function:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ Infinity\  /; \ n\  < \ 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[\(-1\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox["\[Infinity]",
      DirectedInfinity[ 1]]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[0]\  = \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[n_Integer]\  := \ n\ *\ fac[n - 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(fac[6]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(720\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Definition for the greatest common divisor:  the greatest common divisor \
",
  StyleBox["d ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "is the largest number that divides both ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   The definition below is based on a theorem in number theory which \
says, if for integer ",
  StyleBox["a,b,c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " such that |",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "| < ",
  StyleBox["b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and \n                      ",
  StyleBox["a = k b + c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",\nthen gcd[",
  StyleBox["a,b",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "] = gcd[",
  StyleBox["b,c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "].  Doing this (division) until remainder ",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " becomes zero. This is called Euclidean algorithm.                      "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(gcd[a_, \ 0]\  := \ a\), "\n", 
    \(gcd[a_, \ b_] := \ gcd[b, \ Mod[a, b]]\ \)}], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(gcd[3, 6]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["What about gcd[...] with one, three or more arguments?", "Text"],

Cell["\<\

Define our own differentiation algorithm
\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Let's agree that we do only partial differentiation, so that dif[expr, x] \
will be zero unless expr contains x explicitly.  To expression this fact, we \
can say:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[f_, \ x_]\  := \ 0\  /; \ FreeQ[f, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[a, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["We program in the linearity of differentiation:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[\ f_\  + \ g_, \ x_]\  := \ dif[f, x]\  + \ dif[g, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[\ a_\ *\ g_, \ x_]\  := \ 
      a\ *\ dif[g, x]\  /; \ FreeQ[a, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We know differential of polynomials (actually x^n).", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[x, x]\  := \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(dif[x_^n_, \ x_]\  := \ 
      n*x^\((n - 1)\)\  /; \ \((FreeQ[n, x])\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Rule-Based Programming: operator overloading", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Suppose we like to use the notation  S \[Or] T to denote union of two sets S \
and T, and S \[And] T to mean the intersection of the two sets.  Since the \
two special symbol \[And] and \[Or] is used for logical operations on the \
truth values True and False, can we use them for set operation?   We'll use a \
list to represent a set.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a}\  \[And] \ {a, b, c}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a} && {a, b, c}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Since the operation is not defined, we get back the input.  Here is a \
definition that \"overload\" the operator \[And] and \[Or].\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Unprotect[And, \ Or]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(List[x___]\  \[And] \ List[y___]\  := \ 
      Intersection[List[x], List[y]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\  \[And] \ {a, e}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(List[x___]\  \[Or] \ List[y___]\  := \ 
      Union[List[x], \ List[y]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\  || \ {a, e, r}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c, e, r}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, \ b}\  && \ {}\  || \ {c, d}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({c, d}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
WindowSize->{1016, 668},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

